  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/assets/dcac814ed1c1caf5a8796a25ccee57cc5fd988d10f24503cbf0755aeaedb5e2c.png)\n\nFind the values of $t$ at which the curve $x=8t,y=-{t}^{2}$ intersects the line $\\frac{1}{2}x-y=0,$ as shown above.",
  "worked_solution": "Notice that the line and curve intersect twice, so we are expecting two solutions.\n\nTo find the coordinates of the intersection points, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $t.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x-y & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(8t)-(-{t}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 4t+{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ t(4+t) & =0 \\\\ t & =-4,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
